   "noun": "tabako",
   "particle": "o",
   "verb": "suu",
   "verbJa": "吸う",
   "verbSense": "breathe in, suck in (air, smoke)",
   "englishVerb": "smoke",
   "trap": "Dictionaries give kitsuen suru for 'smoke', but that is the word on signs and forms (kitsuenjo, kin'en); in speech you inhale the tobacco: tabako (o) suu. kemuri names only the smoke itself.",
   "partners": [
    "iki (breath)",
    "kuuki (air)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Used to ask about smoking and to say whether you smoke: tabako suu? (do you smoke?), sutte mo ii? (mind if I smoke?). Casual speech drops o.",
   "notes": "Restaurants ask kitsuen seki ka kin'en seki (smoking or non-smoking seat?); kin'en on a sign means no smoking. tabako o nomu is dated (older speakers); don't use it.",
   "example": {
