   "noun": "osake",
   "particle": "ga",
   "verb": "yowai",
   "verbJa": "弱い",
   "verbSense": "weak, having little strength or tolerance",
   "englishVerb": "hold",
   "trap": "English has no set phrase here, so learners fall back on amari nomenai (fine, but blunt). Japanese says the drinker is weak: osake ga yowai (or osake ni yowai). Careful: kono osake tsuyoi is about the drink (high proof), not the drinker.",
   "partners": [
    "asa (mornings: asa ga yowai, not a morning person)",
    "i (stomach: i ga yowai, a delicate stomach)",
    "karada (health: karada ga yowai, sickly)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Said about yourself to turn down drinks gently (osake yowai n desu) or about someone who gets drunk fast. Casual speech drops ga.",
   "notes": "osake ni yowai is equally fine; osake ga tsuyoi is the opposite. With conditions only ni works: samusa ni yowai (can't take the cold), atsusa ni yowai (can't take the heat), norimono ni yowai (get travel-sick); samusa ga yowai would mean the cold is mild.",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "osake yowai kara, ippai dake ni shitoku.",
    "ja": "お酒弱いから、一杯だけにしとく。",
    "english": "I'm a lightweight, so I'll just have one."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "osake yowai n de, ippai dake ni shitokimasu.",
    "ja": "お酒弱いんで、一杯だけにしときます。",
    "english": "I'm not much of a drinker, so I'll just have the one."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-osake-ga-tsuyoi",
   "romaji": "osake ga tsuyoi",
   "polite": "osake ga tsuyoi desu",
   "english": "can hold one's drink",
   "ja": "お酒が強い",
   "kana": "おさけがつよい",
   "politeJa": "お酒が強いです",
   "noun": "osake",
   "particle": "ga",
   "verb": "tsuyoi",
   "verbJa": "強い",
   "verbSense": "strong; with something you take or face, able to handle it",
   "englishVerb": "hold",
   "trap": null,
   "partners": [
    "kikai (machines: kikai ni tsuyoi, good with tech)",
    "samusa (the cold: samusa ni tsuyoi)",
    "puresshaa (pressure: puresshaa ni tsuyoi)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Said about people at drinking parties, often as surprised praise: osake tsuyoi ne! (you can really drink!), or as a question, osake tsuyoi n desu ka? Casual speech drops ga, and osake ni tsuyoi is equally common.",
   "notes": "osake ga yowai is the opposite. With the drink as the subject, kono osake tsuyoi means the drink is high-proof. Someone who never seems to get drunk is jokingly called a zaru (a sieve).",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "osake tsuyoi ne. zenzen kao ni denai.",
    "ja": "お酒強いね。全然顔に出ない。",
    "english": "You can really drink. It doesn't even show on your face."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "osake ga tsuyoi desu ne. zenzen kao ni demasen ne.",
    "ja": "お酒が強いですね。全然顔に出ませんね。",
    "english": "You can really drink. It doesn't show on your face at all."
   }
  }
